#%PAM-1.0
#
# PAM policy for cusk-lock, the session locker.
#
# Its own service rather than borrowing `login` or `system-auth` directly, for
# two reasons.
#
# First, so this file exists at all. cusk-lock resolves its service against
# files on disk and falls back to system-auth, because naming a service with no
# file does not fail open — PAM falls through to `other`, which is pam_deny, and
# the result is a lock screen that rejects every correct password. Shipping the
# file removes that path.
#
# Second, so a site can change the policy for *unlocking a screen* without
# changing the policy for logging in. Adding a fingerprint module here is a
# reasonable thing to want; adding it to system-auth is a much larger decision.
#
# auth and account only. A screen unlock resumes a session that is already
# running: there is no session to open, and `session` stacks have side effects —
# pam_lastlog writes a login record, pam_motd prints — that are wrong for
# something that never logged in. `password` is for changing credentials, which
# this cannot do.
auth        include     system-auth
account     include     system-auth
